<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1917 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Over There</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A cheerful American marching song of 1917, written to get young men to join the army in the First World War.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>George M. Cohan</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>George M. Cohan</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Americans of the First World War</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>written 7 April 1917; first recorded 6 June 1917</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by George M. Cohan, published 1917 (Leo Feist))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>George M. Cohan, an American entertainer, playwright, songwriter, singer, dancer, director and producer, wrote &quot;Over There&quot; in April 1917. According to the Library of Congress he wrote it on his way to work on 7 April, the day after the United States abandoned its policy of non-intervention and joined the Allied Powers in the First World War. It was meant to rally American men to enlist in the American Expeditionary Forces and fight the Central Powers. Its opening verse is derived from Monroe Rosenfeld's song &quot;Johnny, Get Your Gun&quot; of 1886, and its rhythm is based on a three-note bugle call.</p><p>Cohan chose Nora Bayes to give the first performance in June 1917, but the Peerless Quartet recorded the song first, on 6 June, for Columbia Records; Bayes's recording followed on 13 July. Early tests with soldiers met an indifferent reception, but the public took to the song, and Billy Murray, the American Quartet and Arthur Fields recorded it too. President Woodrow Wilson called it &quot;a genuine inspiration to all American manhood&quot;, and by the end of 1918 more than two million copies of the sheet music had been sold. In 1940 the United States Congress awarded Cohan a special medal for it, and Bayes's recording was entered in the Grammy Hall of Fame in 2008.</p><p>The song was revived during and after the Second World War. It was little used during the Vietnam War but came back into use after the September 11 attacks and through the war on terror. In June 2026 CBS News named it among 250 essential American songs of the past 250 years.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic, brisk and confident call to young American men to enlist and go to fight in the war in Europe.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Johnnie get your gun,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Get your gun, get your gun,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Take it on the run,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On the run, on the run,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hear them calling you and me</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Every son of liberty</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurry right away,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>No delay, go today,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Make your daddy glad</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To have had such a lad,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Tell your sweetheart not to pine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To be proud her boy's in line.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Over there</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Over there</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Send the word, send the word over there</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That the Yanks are coming,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The Yanks are coming,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The drums rum-tumming everywhere.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So prepare</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Say a pray'r</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Send the word, send the word to beware.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We'll be over,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We're coming over,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And we won't come back</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Till it's over over there.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: George M. Cohan (1917). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Over_There_(1917)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
