<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Peerless Quartet</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1917</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nora Bayes</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1917</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording (Grammy Hall of Fame, 2008)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Go.Compare advertisements, sung by Wynne Evans</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2009 onward</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Yankee Division March (Kanonenfieber, with Trevor Strnad)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in, as its introduction</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Johnny, Get Your Gun (Monroe Rosenfeld)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1886</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the opening verse derived from it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Three-note bugle call</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its rhythm</span></li></ul></section>
